{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>Stretch out your hand toward the heavens.</b> And similarly in the account, ״ויט משה את ידו על השמים״ — in both of them it should have said אל השמים ('toward the heavens'), for Moses did not stretch his hand literally על השמים ('upon the heavens') as he did upon the sea, of which it is written, ״ויט משה את ידו על הים״." "<b>But</b> it is possible to interpret that since the celestial spheres are material and all the workings of nature are included within them, therefore it says על השמים, for the power of the sign and the portent is above the powers of the spheres, which are nature, and above them the domain of nature does not hold — only the letters of the הויה (the Divine Name) hold there. And because of this it mentions על השמים with ויהי, for the letters of the הויה are upon the heavens. And you will not find that Scripture mentions על השמים except in two plagues alone, and they are hail and darkness: Scripture says of them, ״נטה את ידך על השמים ויהי ברד״, ״נטה ידך על השמים ויהי חשך״ — to say that even though they flowed forth from the heavens and are themselves in the manner of worldly occurrences, the power of the miracle in them is above the heavens. And from this it mentions in each one an expression of הויה: ״ויהי ברד״ and ״ויהי חשך״ — which are the letters of the הויה, above nature. And it is well known that through the letters of the הויה a person can alter nature. And the fact that it mentions here, in the plague of darkness, ״ויט משה את ידו״, while above, in the plague of hail, it mentions ״ויט משה את מטהו״ — all goes to one place: for in the plague of hail Moses drew the power of Atzilut from the staff, alluded to in the alef-bet of ט״ן י״ם, and this is the Malkhut that split the sea before Moses; and from this it immediately mentions ״ה' נתן קולות וברד״, which is like the expression (Genesis 19:24): ״ה' המטיר על סדום״, and likewise it says immediately ״וימטר ה' ברד על ארץ מצרים״. And from there too he drew power for the plague of darkness; therefore it mentions יד in it, for the great Hand and the great Fire are called by this name, from which this darkness was taken, and therefore the Egyptians were judged in it — and also the wicked of Israel — with the attribute of Judgment." "<b>And you will find</b> in the parashah three occurrences of חשך: ״ויהי חשך״, ״וימש חשך״, ״ויהי חשך אפלה״ — to hint that there are three kinds of darkness, and they are: עלטה, אפלה, ערפל. עלטה is the darkness of בין הבתרים (the covenant between the pieces), as it is said (Genesis 15): ״ועלטה היה״. אפלה is the darkness of Egypt, as it is said: ״ויהי חשך אפלה״. ערפל is the darkness of the giving of the Torah, as it is said (Exodus 20:18): ״ומשה נגש אל הערפל״." "<b>ואפלה וימש חשך.</b> He explained: ויחשיך חשך — and it is from the root of אמש, and the intent is that the darkness would darken upon them more than the darkness of night. Or וימש may be from the root of (Exodus 13): ״לא ימיש״ — an expression of removal, meaning: after the darkness of night is removed. And from this we learn that the plague of darkness began at the light of morning, after the night had passed — for had it begun at night, the Egyptians would have thought that the night had simply been lengthened to the span of three days, and there would have been no publicizing of the sign; and in order that it be publicized to all, the plague did not strike until the darkness of night was removed, and it became clear to all that it was day, and then the darkness came. And from this Onkelos translated: בתר דיעדי קבל ליליא — 'after the night had preceded.' And so Elihu explained (Job 34): ״לכן יכיר מעבדיהם״, and ״והפך לילה וידכאו״ — meaning, He turns the light of morning into night and crushes the wicked. And it says (ibid.): ״תחת רשעים ספקם״ — in a place where they see — meaning the wicked, who are the Egyptians: ספקם תחתם, they were all plunged in their places, in the manner of ״ולא קמו איש מתחתיו״, in some place where they see — meaning in the place of light." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> ״וימש חשך״ — He put ממש (substance) in it. They made it from the root of (Deuteronomy 28): ״ממשש בצהרים״. This is what Scripture says (Psalms 105): ״שלח חשך ויחשיך״. To what may the matter be compared? To a king whose servant offended him, and he said to one of his servants: Go and strike him fifty. He went and struck him a hundred and added of his own. So likewise, may His great Name be blessed: He sent darkness upon Egypt, and darkness of His own was added — hence ״שלח חשך ויחשיך״. ״ויהי חשך אפלה״ — from where was the darkness? Rabbi Yehudah and Rabbi Nehemiah. Rabbi Yehudah says: from the darkness above, as it is said (Psalms 18): ״ישת חשך״. Rabbi Nehemiah says: from the darkness of Gehenna, as it is said (Job 10): ״ארץ עיפתה כמו אפל צלמות ולא סדרים״, etc. Seven days the darkness endured: as it is said, ״ויהי חשך אפלה בכל ארץ מצרים שלשת ימים״, ״לא ראו איש את אחיו ולא קמו איש מתחתיו שלשת ימים״ — others — behold, six days: three days it was light and three days it was heavy darkness; the one standing could not sit, the one sitting could not stand. And the seventh day is written (Exodus 14): ״ויהי הענן והחשך חשך למצרים ואור לישראל״ — in every place where they are. And from this it says במושבותיהם, for Scripture should properly have said ״ולכל בני ישראל היה אור בארץ גשן״, according to its usage everywhere with כל; but it mentioned במושבותיהם to teach that even in the house of an Egyptian where an Israelite was, there was light for the Israelite." "<b>And in the explanation</b> of this Midrash — the cause of the plague of darkness and the tangibility (ממש) placed within it is to be understood as follows: the sun was not darkened in its sphere; rather, it stood in its light and its strength all the days of darkness as on the other days, and the world proceeded according to its custom. But the matter was this: the air throughout the entire land of Egypt was blackened, for its pathways were blocked, and HaShem shut them off from the entry of light into them. For it is known that the light of the heavens enters and penetrates the air, and just as sound enters and penetrates it until it reaches the ears of the hearer, so too the light of the sun enters and penetrates through the pathways of the air and along its routes, until the one who sees it perceives it as bright and radiant; and the entry of sunlight into the air is the cause of our seeing the light of the sun. And the Holy One, blessed be He, nullified this cause in Egypt all the days of the darkness, and prevented the sunlight from entering the air — and then the air turned black, and they could not see the light of the sun or the stars, because the pathways of the air and its routes were closed to the entry of light into them. And after the air turned black, the Holy One, blessed be He, made it so heavy (כ״כ) that it acquired tangibility (ממש), and they could feel it by touch; and one who was seated could not rise, nor could he extend his arms, and if they were extended he could not draw them back to himself. And there was light for Israel, because the pathways of light were not closed to them, and the light entered the air in their settlements, and they could see. And this was the cause of the darkness for Egypt and the light for Israel."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {1 "Divine Name הויה rendered by transliteration throughout per voice rules; the kabbalistic allusions (Atzilut, Malkhut, ט״ן י״ם) are left cryptic as required.", 4 "Lemma tag reads 'ובמדרש' but the method-header convention requires 'By way of the Midrash' for על דרך המדרש; here the text opens directly with that heading rather than the standard formula. Comment 3's lemma in the source begins with 'ואפלה' prefixed to 'וימש חשך'; included verbatim in the bold head."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:58:12.387113481Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}